change:
  id: CHG-021
  title: Make the fitness function discriminate
  status: proposed
  intent: Give behavioral_safety a real evidence source and stop erasing a failed candidate's magnitude, so two candidates can be told apart and a population can choose which failure to mutate from.
  audience_level: developer
  relates_to:
    - CON-002
    - PAT-004
    - ARCH-001
    - SYS-001

deltas:
  - op: MODIFIED
    capability: mutation-fitness-loop
    requirement: The weighted sum distinguishes candidates, and a failed candidate retains a comparable magnitude while the promote decision stays binary.
    scenarios:
      - id: S1
        when: an operator declares safety probes and some pass
        then: behavioral_safety is the pass fraction of the declared probes
        and:
          - a probe that produced no evidence counts as failed, the rule the gates already apply
          - a check the operator did not declare a probe does not count towards the fraction
      - id: S2
        when: a declared probe fails
        then: the objective falls and the candidate is not discarded for it
        and:
          - probes are withheld from the deterministic-checks gate, or a probe would gate rather than grade
      - id: S3
        when: no probes are declared
        then: the objective stays at 1.0 and the run is unchanged
      - id: S4
        when: two candidates fail a gate having passed different numbers of behaviour cases
        then: the one that passed more records the higher score, though both are discarded
      - id: S5
        when: a candidate fails a gate and its objectives would otherwise sum above the promotion threshold
        then: it is still discarded, because the decision is gated and a magnitude never buys a promotion
      - id: S6
        when: a declared probe's script exists in the target
        then: it is executed alongside the behaviour cases, so the objective measures a result rather than an absence
      - id: S7
        when: a probe is withheld from the gate so that it grades
        then: it remains in the recorded evidence, so the audit trail can name the probe that lowered the score
        and:
          - everything durable downstream is written from the scored result, so a probe dropped there would vanish from SQLite, the ledger, evolutionary memory and MCP
          - withholding narrows the gate only, never the record
      - id: S8
        when: a discarded candidate records a higher score than a promoted one
        then: evolutionary memory still ranks the promoted candidate above it
        and:
          - decision partitions the ordering and magnitude only breaks ties inside a partition, per CON-002
      - id: S9
        when: a category's best representative was already taken by an earlier selection pass
        then: the category slot reaches a record the selection does not already hold
        and:
          - decision-first ordering offers the same promoted record to every pass, so a slot spent on a duplicate would leave the category unrepresented while reporting it filled

acceptance:
  - scenario: S1
    test_id: deterministic/BehaviouralSafetyScoreTest.isThePassFractionOfTheDeclaredProbes
    layer: unit
  - scenario: S2
    test_id: deterministic/BehaviouralSafetyScoreTest.aFailingProbeLowersTheScoreWithoutDiscarding
    layer: unit
  - scenario: S3
    test_id: deterministic/BehaviouralSafetyScoreTest.staysAtOneWhenNoProbesAreDeclared
    layer: unit
  - scenario: S4
    test_id: deterministic/FailedCandidateMagnitudeTest.aNearMissOutscoresATotalMissAlthoughBothDiscard
    layer: unit
  - scenario: S5
    test_id: deterministic/FailedCandidateMagnitudeTest.theDecisionIsStillBinaryAndUntradeable
    layer: unit
  - scenario: S6
    test_id: cli/EvolveContractAcceptanceTest.aFailingSafetyProbeLowersTheScoreWithoutDiscarding
    layer: acceptance
  - scenario: S7
    test_id: deterministic/BehaviouralSafetyScoreTest.aWithheldProbeStaysInTheRecordedEvidence
    layer: unit
  - scenario: S8
    test_id: deterministic/LineageNoveltyMemoryPolicyTest.aHigherScoringFailureDoesNotTakeAChampionSlotFromAPromotion
    layer: unit
  - scenario: S9
    test_id: deterministic/LineageNoveltyMemoryPolicyTest.aNoveltySlotIsNotSpentOnARecordAlreadySelected
    layer: unit

tasks:
  - id: T1
    slice: score behavioral_safety as the declared probes' pass fraction, withheld from the checks gate
    status: completed
  - id: T2
    slice: stop zeroing a failed candidate's score so the magnitude survives, leaving the decision gated
    status: completed
  - id: T3
    slice: expose --safety-probe and execute probe scripts alongside behaviour cases
    status: completed
  - id: T4
    slice: update the golden corpus, the property test and the assertions that pinned the zeroing rather than the invariant
    status: completed
  - id: T5
    slice: correct the README, which described the objective as the literal 1.0 and did not mention that a failed candidate keeps its score
    status: completed
  - id: T6
    slice: keep withheld probes in the recorded evidence by narrowing the gate rather than deleting the check, so the audit trail survives
    status: completed
  - id: T7
    slice: order evolutionary memory by decision before score, so a high-scoring failure cannot take a champion slot from a promotion
    status: completed
  - id: T8
    slice: record the old/new discard-score semantics boundary as RISK-007 and the score-is-not-a-verdict rule in CON-002
    status: completed
  - id: T9
    slice: stop a category slot being spent on an already-selected record, which decision-first ordering turned from an occasional collision into a certainty
    status: completed

non_goals:
  - ranking or population, which this unblocks but does not implement
  - reclaiming reliability, which needs repeated execution to mean anything
  - the richer score type CON-002 implies for ordering across severity classes

risks:
  - a discarded candidate can now record a score above the promotion threshold, which reads oddly in a journal or ledger until you know the decision is what promotes. The README says so explicitly
  - outcomes recorded before this change stored 0.0 for every discard, so the same field carries two meanings either side of it. Decision-first ordering keeps old and new records from competing except among failures, and RISK-007 tracks the residue
  - any future consumer that ranks on score alone reintroduces the champion-slot defect. CON-002 now states the rule; nothing mechanically enforces it until the FitnessScore type exists
  - scores are stored rounded to two decimals, so two near misses differing by less than 0.005 record identically. Tolerable when comparing a handful of candidates, and it needs revisiting before a population large enough for that to collide
  - reliability remains pinned at 1.0 for any eligible candidate, so task_success and parsimony carry the discrimination among promotable candidates. The floor problem is narrowed, not solved
  - probes are executed as ordinary checks in the candidate worktree, so a probe script can do anything a behaviour case can. They are graded rather than gated, which means a compromised probe lowers a score rather than forcing a promotion
